From: CurtAdams@aol.com
Date: Wed, 9 Nov 1994 08:19:08 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-140] Democracy

Steve Whitrow (sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk) writes:

>CurtAdams@aol.com wrote:
>>Steve Whitrow (sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk) writes:
>>>Democracy is like two wolves and ninety-eight sheep voting
>>>on what to have for dinner.  The good news is that there are
>>>three or four choices on the menu.  The bad news is that >>>each dish
includes mutton [...]
>>
>>In many countries, more people get money from the >>government than pay
money to the government.  
>>2 wolves and 1 sheep is a sadly appropriate analogy.

>I question the assumption that the majority of people benefit >from
government, compared to no government.  The massive >cost of government would
mean that the gainers could only >gain a tiny fraction of losses incurred by
the losers.  In >Britain, for example, government currently costs 291.8
>billion pounds ($470 billion) per annum, which is about $410 >weekly per
worker, $160 per capita, or about $220 per >eligible voter. Let's say 51% of
voters are better off by $10 >weekly, thus the other 49% are worse off by
$459 on average.  >Or if 67% are better off by $20 per week, 33% lose by
$707.  >In fact, a minority profit by much more than $20 per week, >lessening
the possibility of the remaining 51% / 67% >benefiting and /or increasing the
losers' losses.  

Costs of government are not simply lost.  In most Western countries, most
government money goes to transfer payments, generally with relatively little
spent in the process.  (Pensions,  social security, and interest payments,
with little overhead, consume far more money than welfare or the dole, which
are quite wasteful.)  A good deal of the rest goes to salaries, which again
benefit someone.  Although the benefits of the money spent would be greater
through free market allocation, a lot of people get a lot of benefit.

>The next step is to deduct the cost of obtaining comparable >services in a
free society.  For starters, many of the >"services" of government are
services that many would >choose to forgo.  Eg, being prohibited from the
right to ingest >substances into one's own body or not as the case may be,
and >as a side effect being more likely to be shot, mugged, burgled, >etc.
Next we have to decide how much prices would be >lowered by free competition
in a consumers' market compared >to a monopoly in each market (socialised law
and order, >socialised charity, socialised currency, socialised medicine,
>nanny state, etc).  In one system, consumers may freely >choose which legal
agency to sign up with, which security >services to use (or opt for personal
defence), who to sell >their labour to, where to invest in order to insure
against a >rainy day, which charity to support in order to help the
>genuinely needy, etc.  The other system has monopoly >"service" providers,
these being funded by stolen money >seized by common criminals who run
something like a chain >letter or pyramid selling scam in that the
institution can only >consume wealth, but with the added feature that
>participation in the scheme is compulsory.

>If we predict prices to drop by less than 50% and allow a >little more for
unwanted services this suggests a cost of >government per voter of $110 per
week, although I'd place a >higher value on freedom, and OPEC was able to
quadruple the >oil price in 1973.  With the $110 per voter average net loss,
>if 67% are better off by $10, then 33% lose $354 per week.  It >sounds quite
absurd, but so does putting up with an extortion >racket that takes $21320
annually per worker.  

Goverment efficiency losses are undoubtedly huge, but they cannot be
calculated for the same reasons Mises used to show a central planner cannot
make well-informed decisions.  


>I suspect that any studies which count people whose welfare >income slightly
exceeds their income tax outlay haven't >allowed for all the other costs of
government.  Such as paying >higher prices for goods and services as a
consequence of >corporate taxation, taxes incurred on transportation costs,
>costs of complying with regulations, sales taxes added to >these prices,
possible special taxes added before the sales >tax (e.g. gasoline), and
savings taxes such as inflation.  [It's >been calculated that the European
Commission (formerly the >"Common Market") alone has cost the average British
worker >some 11,000 pounds so far in higher food prices.]  And
>these are just the tangible costs.

Nope, these studies don't take that into account.  It would be a good idea to
have one that does take into account easy-to-quantify costs like food price
increases from agricultural policy.  This would have the effect of showing
that some apparent wolves are really sheep in the long term and might affect
their behavior.

Still, many government beneficiaries are purely wolves.  Government
employees, long-term welfare dependents, retirees dependent on social
security and government bond income, employees of firms that are tightly tied
to the government, like defense contractors, etc., all get most or all of
their income from the government.  They might do better in a free market,
doing different things, but eliminating the government would leave them, in
their eyes, more or less destitute.  Hence they tend to be very strongly
supportive of government intervention which benefits them.  In the US,
virtually all federal money goes to national defense, social
security/medicare, and interest on the debt- i.e., appeasing the above
mentioned wolves.

